  "Question": "The product, \\(\\log_a b\\cdot\\log_b a\\) is equal to:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(1\\)",
    "B": "\\(a\\)",
    "C": "\\(b\\)",
    "D": "\\(ab\\)",
    "E": "none of these"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "Using the same base for both logarithms reveals the cancellation. For the given real logarithms to be defined, \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a,b\\ne1\\). By change of base,\n\\[\n\\log_a b\\cdot\\log_b a\n=\\frac{\\log_{10}b}{\\log_{10}a}\n \\cdot\\frac{\\log_{10}a}{\\log_{10}b}\n=1.\n\\]\nNeither denominator is zero, because neither \\(a\\) nor \\(b\\) equals \\(1\\). Thus the product is \\(1\\), choice A.",
